  "Ça a commencé comme ça."

  A complex number is the sum of a real number and an imaginary number.
  It takes the form:
               ____
    z  =  x + √ —1 ·y

    where x is the real part of z:       Re(z)  =  x
    and y is the imaginary part of z:    Im(z)  =  y

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_number

  Let's imagine a Cartesian plane
  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cartesian_coordinate_system
  whose horizontal axis (abscissa) is the set of real numbers,
  and whose vertical axis (ordinate) is the set pure imaginary numbers.
  This plane is called the complex plane.
  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_plane
  Now imagine we plot z on this plane.
  It shows how any complex number can be regarded
  as a vector joining the origin of both axis
  and the point with coordinates  ( x = Re(z) , y = Im(z) ) .
  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vector_(mathematics_and_physics)

  This vector traces the diagonal of a rectangle
  with two sides = Re(z)
  and two sides = Im(z).
  The vector z splits this rectangle in two identical right triangles
  whose hypotenuse is the length (magnitude) of the vector.
  This hypotenuse is called the absolute value (or magnitude) of the complex number z
  and is notated |z| ——  sometimes abs(z).

  The theorem of Pythagoras gives:
             ________________
    |z|  =  √ Re(z)² + Im(z)²

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pythagorean_theorem
  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pythagoras
  /*
    Notice that if  Im(z) = 0
    then  |z| = √{ Re(z)² }  which results in ignoring z's sign.
    Which is often mistaken for the actual definition of a number's absolute value.
    It stops being valid once said number has an imaginary part,
    while the definition given by complex analysis applies to purely real numbers.
    This coincides with the fact that real numbers are a subset of complex numbers.
  */

  Let's call θ ("theta", not "zero"...)
  the angle formed by the vector z and the real (horizontal) axis.
  θ is called the argument
  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argument_(complex_analysis)
  of the complex number z
  and is notated  arg(z) .

  Since  Re(z)  is its adjacent side
  and since  Im(z)  is its opposite side:

    tan(θ)  =  Im(z)/Re(z)

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trigonometric_functions

  And thus:

    arg(z)  =  arctan( Im(z)/Re(z) )

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inverse_trigonometric_functions

  or rather:

    atan2( Im(z) , Re(z) )

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atan2

  /*
    atan2:
      The regular arctan function ——
      ——  atan(x) ,  tan⁻¹(x)  ——
      —— tends to —π/2 when x tends to —∞;
      it tends to +π/2 when x tends to +∞.
      It only outputs angles in the North-East and South-East quadrants.

      The argument of a complex number is in the range ]—π , +π].
      It spans a full turn (4 quadrants):
        #  South-West, ]—π , —π/2]:
            Re(z)  and  Im(z)  are both negative.
            Re(z)  crosses zero at —π/2.
        #  South-East, ]—π/2 , 0]:
            Re(z)  is positive,  Im(z)  is negative.
            Im(z)  crosses zero at zero.
        #  North-East, ]0 , +π/2]:
            Re(z)  and  Im(z)  are both positive.
            Re(z)  crosses zero at +π/2.
        #  North-West, ]+π/2 , +π]:
            Re(z)  is negative,  Im(z)  is positive.
            Im(z)  crosses zero at +π.
        #  Repeat.

      If you feed the ratio directly to arctan,
      you've lost track of which term is responsible for the sign.
      All arctan can possibly know
      is whether the denominator and the numerator have the same sign.
      By having two separate variables,
      atan2(y , x)  fixes that.
      E.g.:
        You feed your ratio to arctan and get π/3.
        (So North-East quadrant.)
        But you know that both terms were negative.
        So you know that you should be in the South-West quadrant.
        That's a 2-quadrant jump. So half a circle. So ±π.
        +π would outreach the boundaries ]—π , +π]:

           π           π + 3π      4
          ——— + π  =  ———————  =  ——— π
           3             3         3

        so you know the argument is:    π/3 — π  =  —2π/3

      Also: if  x = 0 ,
      arctan(y/x)  is fed an exploding division by zero...
      atan2(y , x)  fixes that too.

      Don't worry too much about actual computation.
      This is Chapter I.
      We'll have plenty of opportunities to dig a lot deeper around the unit-circle.
      (Notably in chapters VII and VIII.)

      For now:
      square root of a negative number ?  =>  imaginary  =>  vertical
      If not ?  =>  real  =>  horizontal
      Two sets, two signs, ergo: four quadrants.

      Cardinal points:
        #  South (—π/2):
            purely imaginary, negative.
        #  East (0):
            purely real, positive.
        #  North (+π/2):
            purely imaginary, positive.
        #  West (π):
            purely real, negative.

      Then there's the case:  Re(z)  =  Im(z)  =  0 .
      Theoretically the argument is undefined
      (the magnitude has no length, the angle could be anything).
      But for the sake of continuity, usually:

        arg(0)  =  atan2(0 , 0)  =  0

    I don't know exactly how atan2 is implemented in cmath and such
    (my uneducated guess is: a series of if/else statements);
    but, after tweaking Desmos a bit,
    I came up with this formula,
    which works for all quadrants and critical values:

      double wc.atan2 (double a, double b)
      {
        return
          std::fmod(
            std::atan(b / a)  +  M_PI * std::max( 0.0 , boost::math::sign(-a) )
            ,
            -M_PI
          )
          +  M_PI * std::max( 0.0 , boost::math::sign(b) );
      }

    This outputs NaN when a and b are both zero.    //  "Theoretically the argument is undefined
    Which it should...                              //  (the magnitude has no length,
    ... ok:                                         //  the angle could be anything)."

      double wc.atan2 (double a, double b)
      {
        return
          (a == 0.0  &&  b == 0.0)  ?
            0.0
          :
            std::fmod(
              std::atan(b / a)  +  M_PI * std::max( 0.0 , boost::math::sign(-a) )
              ,
              -M_PI
            )
            +  M_PI * std::max( 0.0 , boost::math::sign(b) );
      }

    Still: don't implement that ——
    —— it puts far too much confidence in IEEE 754 floating-point division by zero.
    Don't go pushing fragile code out there in the wasteland
    just because some guy with OCDs doesn't like conditional branching.
    But at least now you get to see atan2
    as neither a 6-line shopping-list
    nor a black-box incantation.
  */

  We also know that:

    cos(θ)  =  adjacent / hypotenuse

  and:

    sin(θ)  =  opposite / hypotenuse

  which means:

    adjacent  =  hypotenuse · cos(θ)

  and:

    opposite  =  hypotenuse · sin(θ)

  Remember that:
    #  adjacent is  Re(z)
    #  opposite is  Im(z)
    #  hypotenuse is |z|
    #  θ is  arg(z)
                            ____
  Also that  z  =  Re(z) + √ —1 ·Im(z) .

  Then:

    Re(z)  =  |z|·cos( arg(z) )
    Im(z)  =  |z|·sin( arg(z) )

  Then:

    z  =  |z| { cos( arg(z) ) + √(—1) sin( arg(z) ) }

    This expression is called the polar form of the complex number z.

  /*
    Complex numbers can be regarded as vectors.
    Vectors also have a polar form.
    The notation:  v  =  ( r , ∠θ )
    means:  v is a vector of magnitude r and angle θ.
    If v is represented with the complex number z,
    then:  |z| = r  and  arg(z) = θ .
  */

  The absolute value |z| of z is sometimes also called "modulus" or "magnitude".
  The argument  arg(z)  of z is sometimes also called "phase".



===================================================================================================
#02  Euler's formula
===================================================================================================
            ____
  Let  j = √ —1   and  x  be a real number,
  then Euler's formula states that:

    exp(jx)  =  cos(x) + j·sin(x)

  /*
    ↑↑↑ Don't overlook that one. ↑↑↑
    (We'll demonstrate it at some point.)
  */
  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_function
  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler's_formula
  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonhard_Euler

  By making use of the 2 following trigonometric identities:

    cos(—x)  =  cos(x)    and    sin(—x)  =  —sin(x)

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_trigonometric_identities#Reflections,_shifts,_and_periodicity

  Euler's formula can be re-expressed as:

    exp(j · —x)  =  cos(—x) + j·sin(—x)  =  cos(x) — j·sin(x)

  Then [1]:

    exp(jx) — exp(—jx)  =  cos(x) + j·sin(x) — { cos(x) — j·sin(x) }
                        =  2j·sin(x)

  And [2]:

    exp(jx) + exp(—jx)  =  cos(x) + j·sin(x) + { cos(x) — j·sin(x) }
                        =  2·cos(x)

  Solving [1] for  sin(x)  gives:

                exp(jx) — exp(—jx)
    sin(x)  =  ———————————————————
                       2j

  Solving [2] for  cos(x)  gives:

                exp(jx) + exp(—jx)
    cos(x)  =  ————————————————————    [3]
                        2

  Considering the identity:

    cos(θ)  =  sin(θ + π/2)

  equation [3] shows that any real sinusoid
  can be expressed as half the sum
  of two complex exponentials.
  This is interesting because the rules of exponentiation
  are a lot simpler than the rules of trigonometry.
  For instance,
  exp(x)  is its own derivative (and thus anti-derivative) —— by definition.
  /*
    More on that soon enough...
  */
  This makes processes such as integration or differentiation become very trivial,
  compared to the same processes applied to  cos(x)  or  sin(x) .
  (Actually,
  derivatives and antiderivatives
  are not where real trigonometric functions are the trickiest,
  but:  cos(ax + b) , etc.)

  Let:

    z₁  =  exp(jx)/2

  and:

    z₂  =  exp(—jx)/2

  then:

    z₁  =  cos(x)/2  +  j · +sin(x)/2
    z₂  =  cos(x)/2  +  j · —sin(x)/2

  Their respective real (Re) and imaginary (Im) parts are thus:

    Re(z₁)  =  cos(x)/2    ;    Im(z₁)  =  +sin(x)/2
    Re(z₂)  =  cos(x)/2    ;    Im(z₂)  =  —sin(x)/2

    =>  Re(z₁)  =  Re(z₂)    ;    Im(z₁)  =  —Im(z₂)

  z₁ and z₂ form a complex conjugate pair.
  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_conjugate
  (Which means: they have equal real parts and opposite imaginary parts.)
  So any real sinusoid (equation [3])
  can be expressed as the sum of two complex conjugates [a].
  /*
    Complex conjugates are usually denoted
    by a horizontal bar over the number's symbol:
             __
      z₂  =  z₁

    means that z₂ and z₁ are conjugates.
    Sometimes also with an asterisk:

      z₂  =  z₁*

    First option is really not convenient because monospace.
    Second option looks like multiplication in most programming languages ——
    —— or [SPOILER] convolution [/SPOILER].
    So I'll use:

      z₂  =  conj(z₁)

    —— the Desmos way, for practical reasons.
    But know that this is not standard
    and be ready to encounter other conventions.
  */

  // Euler's formula and the polar form
  Let's consider a complex number z
  with argument  arg(z)
  and absolute value  |z| .

  According to Euler's formula:

    exp( j·arg(z) )  =  cos( arg(z) ) + j·sin( arg(z) )

  Recalling the polar form expression:

    z  =  |z|{ cos( arg(z) ) + j·sin( arg(z) ) }

  Combining these two expressions leads to:

    z  =  |z|exp( j·arg(z) )

  I haven't found any documentation on this form
  (perhaps from not digging deep enough...)
  so let's call it the exponential form of the complex number z.

  This means that any complex number in the form:

    ζ  =  α exp(j θ)

  has an absolute value:  |ζ| = α  and an argument:  arg(ζ) = θ

  Then:  Re(ζ) = α·cos(θ)  and:  Im(ζ) = α·sin(θ)

  Now in the special case:  θ = ωt
  where t is the time (in seconds)
  and ω is an angular frequency (in radians per second),
  the exponential form:

    ζ  =  α·exp(jωt)    [4]

  is what is called a COMPLEX SINUSOID —— sometimes a phasor.
  It is also called an analytic signal
  as it has optimal qualities in order to study a system's response to pure sinusoids.
  Indeed comparing equations [3] and [4] reveals that
  if we express a real sinusoid in terms of complex exponentials,
  we end up with two complex sinusoids,
  one of which has a negative frequency;
  while a pure complex sinusoid has its amplitude and phase
  embedded in its constant factor and only involves positive frequencies.
  More on that right away.
  /*
    EDIT:
      It's the chapter's title... better be slightly more precise.
      A complex sinusoid is an analytic signal only if ω is positive.
      That's the complete definition:
        complex-valued function
        AND no negative-frequency components.
      Starting with a real cosine,
      it can be thought of in two ways:
        #  add a π/2 shifted copy of the signal to itself on the imaginary axis;
        #  double the positive-frequency component, remove the negative frequency one.
      Both operations result in the substitution:

        cos(ωt)    <——    cos(ωt) + j·sin(ωt)  =  exp(jωt)

      More details here:  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Analytic_signal#Examples

      It's also pretty convenient when testing trigonometric identities,
      because it embeds the three types of sinusoids.
      E.g.:

        exp( j( x + nπ ) )  =  exp(jx)exp(jπ)ⁿ
                            =  (—1)ⁿ·exp(jx)
                            =  (—1)ⁿ·{ cos(x) + j·sin(x) }
                            =  (—1)ⁿ·cos(x) + j·(—1)ⁿ sin(x)

            ╭  cos(x + nπ)  =  (—1)ⁿ·cos(x)
        =>  ┤
            ╰  sin(x + nπ)  =  (—1)ⁿ·sin(x)

  */

  Before we move on:
  what happens to ζ if α has an imaginary part?

    α  =  |α|exp( j arg(α) )

  then:

    ζ  =  |α|exp( j·arg(α) )exp(jωt)

       =  |α|·exp( j{ ωt + arg(α) } )    [4-bis]



