#03  Amplitude, frequency & phase
===================================================================================================

  [Amplitude, frequency & phase]  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/hwpazzploq

  If t is the time (in seconds),
  ƒ(t)  =  cos(t)  is a sinusoid of:

    Amplitude:    α  =  1
       Period:    T  =  2π  seconds
    Frequency:    f  =  1/2π  Hertz (Hz)
        Phase:    φ  =  0  radian (rad)

  That is,
  ƒ(t)  oscillates from —1 to +1
  through a repetitive pattern of length = 2π
  with an offset = 0.

  Notice that:

    ƒ(t)  =  α·cos(2π f t  +  φ)

  Or, after defining the angular frequency  ω = 2πf  (rad/s) :        //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angular_frequency

    ƒ(t)  =  α·cos( ωt + φ )    [5]

  That is,
  ƒ(t)  oscillates from —α to +α
  through a repetitive pattern of length = 1/ω
  with an offset = φ .

  Note that for φ to be expressed in radians
  (that is as a portion of the modulus 2π),
  the offset t₀ (seconds) must be defined.
  t₀ is the time  ƒ(t)  takes to reach the beginning of an oscillation cycle
  ( ƒ(t)  =  α·cos(0)  =  α )
  for the first time.
  (This implies  |t₀| < T .)

  Then:

    ƒ(t)  =  α·cos( ω(t — t₀) )

  And:

    φ  =  ω · —t₀

  /*
    Remember that  cos(θ)  =  sin(θ + π/2) .
    Which means that the relationship between sine and cosine
    is simply a constant horizontal offset.
    I chose  ƒ(t)  =  cos(t)  as the base sinusoid,
    but  ƒ(t)  =  sin(t)  would be just as valid.
    In such a case  ƒ(t)  =  cos(t + φ)  would have a phase  φ  =  π/2  rad.

    This is a pure matter of convention.
    Since converting one convention into the other is as trivial as an addition,
    which one we choose has few consequences.
    So if one convention simplifies the maths,
    why choose the other? Right?

    We're just about to call back Euler's formula.
    Choosing cosine avoids having j in the denominator.
    I hope now it doesn't feel arbitrary anymore.

    Also: because  sin(0)  =  0 ,
    sine is inherently unable to express constants (with null frequencies);
    cosine, on the other hand,
    outputs 1 at 0 —— which is quite convenient.
    /*
      Theoretically,
      sine would only need a quarter-circle of phase to match cosine,
      but this kind of collides with the expression of φ:

                                           π
        —t₀·0  =  π/2    <=>    t₀  =  — —————
                                          0·2
      ... not super legal.
      Yet this undefined result is a useful information:
      the phase of a constant is an irrelevant concept.
    */
  */

  Using equation [3] to express  ƒ(t)
  in terms of complex exponentials instead of raw trigonometry:

              α                         α
    ƒ(t)  =  ——— exp( j (ωt + φ) )  +  ——— exp( —j (ωt + φ) )
              2                         2

              α·exp(jφ)               α·exp(j · —φ)
    ƒ(t)  =  ——————————— exp(jω)  +  ———————————————— exp(j · —ωt)    [6]
                  2                         2

  Comparing this expression of  ƒ (t) to equation [4]
  shows that a real sinusoid is the sum of two complex sinusoids,
  one of which has a negative frequency.

  Let's define:
                                 α·exp(jφ)
    s  =  jω    and    X(s)  =  ———————————
                                     2

  X(s)  is a complex number in exponential form
  with absolute value (magnitude)  |X(s)|  =  α/2
  and argument  arg( X(s) )  =  φ .

  Then any complex sinusoid in the form:

    ƒ(t)  =  X(s)exp(st)    //  where s is a *pure imaginary* number ( Re(s) = 0 )

  can be considered the positive-frequency-component of a real sinusoid with [b]:

    Amplitude:    α  =  2 |X(s)|
        Phase:    φ  =  arg( X(s) )
    Frequency:    ω  =  s/j  rad/s    or    f  =  s/2πj  Hz

  /*
    X(s)  is called the complex amplitude of  ƒ(t) .
    It embeds the real amplitude and the phase.
    X(s)  is just a symbol.
    The complex sinusoid  A exp(z t)  has a complex amplitude = A
    as long as  Re(z) = 0 .
    (If A is purely real,
    then the phase is zero,
    and complex amplitude = real amplitude.)
  */

  And thus the two (positive and negative) complex sinusoids
  that form a real sinusoid
  have equal amplitude and opposite phase.
  (Opposite phase because we measure it in radians,
  in fact they have the same t₀.)

  /*
    Infinite feedback loop:
      # A real sinusoid is a sum of complex sinusoids.
      # A complex sinusoid is the sum of a real and an imaginary sinusoid.
  */

  There is a process called Hilbert transform
  which applies a different phase shift (± π/2)
  depending on whether the frequency is positive or negative.
  This ends up in doubling the amplitude of positive frequencies
  and cancelling negative frequencies.
  Then real sinusoids have become complex sinusoids.
  (Not that simple if you stop sticking to cos, sin and exp.)

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert_transform

  A very convenient tool indeed.
  But I'd rather find a way to study signals without transforming them
  (even though it's easily reversible).
  So I'll take a different route.
  /*
    EDIT:
      The Hilbert transform turns real signals into analytic signals.
      Considering the chapter's title, it had to be mentioned.
      But the central question of this chapter is not
      "how to build analytic signals" ——
      —— rather: "are they valid, and how are they useful".
      So if you're really interested in it,
      follow the Wikipedia link.
      We won't be addressing it any further here.
  */



