  */
  Since σ is constant, σt is also affine.
  So it's also linear.
  But whether it's growing, decaying or constant
  depends on the value of σ.

    case 1:  σ = 0
      ——>  σt = 0
      it's constant (more precisely: null)
      then  exp(σt) = exp(0) = 1
      and  α(t) = 2·|A(t)|
      and  Laplace = Fourier
      (grossly speaking... I mean writing)

    case 2:  σ < 0
      ——>  σt inherits the sign inversion and becomes linearly decaying over time.
      then  exp(σt)  is exponentially decaying over time.
      Since k is an absolute value, it's positive.
      So it's a positive constant.
      Then  α(t)  is exponentially decaying over time.

    case 3:  σ > 0
      ——>  σt keeps t's sign and remains linearly growing over time.
      then  exp(σt)  is exponentially growing over time.
      Since k is an absolute value, it's positive.
      So it's a positive constant.
      Then  α(t)  is exponentially growing over time.

  We still don't know what σ exactly is.
  But we know that it makes the magnitude of a signal
    #  grow exponentially,
    #  decay exponentially
    #  or remain constant
  depending on whether it's positive, negative or zero.
  And maybe that's all there is to know?

  So if we reunite s,
  this means that, in its Laplace representation,
  a signal has an exponentially growing magnitude when  Re(s) > 0 ;
  an exponentially decaying magnitude when  Re(s) < 0 ;
  and a constant magnitude when  Re(s) = 0 .

  [Sigma]  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/h8zpw4fkp5

  Another way to come to the same conclusion
  would have been to consider one (complex) sinusoid in isolation:

   F(s)exp(st)       F(s)
  —————————————  =  ————— exp(σt)exp(jωt)
       2πj           2πj

  exp(jωt)  is a periodic 2-dimension attenuator,
  F(s)/2πj  is a constant,
  t is linearly growing,
  then case 1, case 2, case 3 &c.
  Since σ is constant over time and across all partials,
  cases 1, 2 and 3 will apply to all partials.

  Why did I ask?
  Genuine curiosity unrelated to anything practical.
  (We'll see about that...)

