    Stupid complaint about complex algebra:

      Complex conjugates are often taught as an ultra—fundamental concept
      when talking complex numbers.
      Pardon my blunt tone, but I tend to consider it short—sighted.

      The concept of conjugation is not binary.
      Well it is for real numbers:
        1 dimension, 2 directions  ——>  2 cases.
      But complex numbers have:
        1 circle, 4 quadrants  ——>  4 cases.

      Let:  z  =  a + jb    //  a and b are strictly positive real numbers.

      Then:
              +z  =  +a + jb
              —z  =  —a — jb
        +conj(z)  =  +a — jb  =  —conj(—z)
        —conj(z)  =  —a + jb  =  +conj(—z)

      or, in exponential form:
              +z  =  +M·exp(+jφ)
              —z  =  —M·exp(+jφ)
        +conj(z)  =  +M·exp(—jφ)  =  —conj(—z)
        —conj(z)  =  —M·exp(—jφ)  =  +conj(—z)

      Or rather, since the magnitude must be positive:

              |+z|  =  √{ (+a)² + (+b)² }  =  √{a² + b²}  =  M
              |—z|  =  √{ (—a)² + (—b)² }  =  √{a² + b²}  =  M
        |+conj(z)|  =  √{ (+a)² + (—b)² }  =  √{a² + b²}  =  M
        |—conj(z)|  =  √{ (—a)² + (+b)² }  =  √{a² + b²}  =  M

      then:

        +z  =  M·cos(φ)  +  j·M·sin(φ)

           ╭  a  =  M·cos(φ)
        => ┤
           ╰  b  =  M·sin(φ)

           ╭         —z  =  —M·cos(φ) — j·M·sin(φ)
           │             =  M· cos(φ ± π) + j·M·sin(φ ± π)
           │
        => ┤  +conj(+z)  =  +M·cos(φ) — j·M·sin(φ)
           │             =  M·cos(—φ) + j·M·sin(—φ)
           │
           │  —conj(+z)  =  —M·cos(φ) + j·M·sin(φ)
           ╰             =  M·cos(±π — φ) + j·M·sin(±π — φ)

